Paragraph (3) Sufficiently clear Paragraph (4) Sufficiently clear

Article 16
(1) Prevention of the occurrence of acts of violence against women and children as referred to in Article 15, shall be implemented by Regional Apparatus whose duties and functions are in the fields of:
a. social affairs;
b. health;
c. education;
d. manpower;
e. population and civil registration;
f. law;
g. women's empowerment and child protection;
h. cooperatives and Micro, Small and Medium Enterprises;
i. mental and spiritual matters; and
j. public peace and order.
(2) Prevention of acts of violence by Regional Apparatus as referred to in paragraph (1), shall be implemented in an integrated and sustainable manner based on the Regional Long-Term Development Plan and the Regional Medium-Term Development Plan.

Article 22
(1) Regional Government may organize partnerships with the business sector in the protection of women and children from acts of violence;
(2) Partnerships as referred to in paragraph (1) are conducted through: a. notification of employment opportunity information for women victims of acts of violence;
b. education and training for women victims of acts of violence; c. educational assistance for women and children victims of acts of violence who have been withdrawn from their education; and
d. fostering and increasing economic independence of women victims of acts of violence.
(3) Partnerships as referred to in paragraph (1) and paragraph (2), are implemented in accordance with the provisions of Laws and Regulations.

Article 10
Every person who hears, sees, or knows of the occurrence of violence against women and children is obligated to make efforts in accordance with the limits of their capabilities to:
a. Prevent and stop the ongoing act of violence;
b. Provide protection to the victim;
c. Provide emergency assistance; and/or
d. Assist in the process of submitting an application for the determination of protection.

Article 3
The objectives of protecting women and children from acts of violence are to:
a. Prevent acts of violence against women and children, including trafficking in persons;
b. Eliminate all forms of violence and exploitation against women and children;
c. Protect and provide a sense of security for women and children;
d. Provide services to women and children who are victims of violence, reporting parties, and witnesses; and
e. Empower women and children who are victims of violence so that they are more empowered physically, psychologically, socially, and economically.

Article 21
(1) Regional Government develops cooperation patterns in order to achieve the objectives of protecting women and children from acts of violence in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations.
(2) Cooperation as referred to in paragraph (1) is conducted between the Regional Government and:
a. Central Government;
b. Provincial Government;
c. Other Regency/City Government;
d. Higher Education Institutions;
e. Social and religious institutions;
f. Non-Governmental Organizations; and/or
g. Media.
(3) Cooperation as referred to in paragraph (2) includes:
a. exchange of data and information;
b. rehabilitation of victims of violence;
c. repatriation and social reintegration; and
d. provision of evidence and witnesses.
(4) Cooperation as referred to in paragraph (2) is implemented in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations

MAYOR OF SEMARANG,

Considering: a. that the fulfillment of constitutional rights and the protection of human rights of women and children against acts of violence is one of the values contained in Pancasila and the 1945 Constitution;
b. that violence against women and children in the City of Semarang continues to increase and spread, causing citizens to be unsafe in carrying out their lives, thus requiring integrated protection efforts;
c. that to provide direction and legal certainty to all those involved in efforts to provide legal protection, regulation is needed regarding the protection of women and children from acts of violence;
d. that based on the considerations as referred to in letter a, letter b, and letter c, it is necessary to establish a Regional Regulation of the City of Semarang concerning the Protection of Women and Children From Acts of Violence.

In View of: 1. Article 18 Paragraph (6) of the 1945 Constitution of the Republic of Indonesia;
2. Law Number 16 of 1950 concerning the Establishment of Large City Regions within the Province of East Java, Central Java, West Java and the Special Region of Yogyakarta;
3. Law Number 23 of 2002 concerning Child Protection (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2002 Number 109, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4235) as amended by Law of the Republic of Indonesia Number 35 of 2014 concerning Amendment to Law Number 23 of 2002 Concerning Child Protection (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2014 Number 297, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5606);
4. Law Number 23 of 2004 concerning the Elimination of Domestic Violence (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2004 Number 95, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4419);
5. Law Number 39 of 2004 concerning the Protection of Indonesian Migrant Workers Abroad (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2004 Number 133, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4445);
6. Law Number 13 of 2006 concerning the Protection of Witnesses and Victims (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2006 Number 64, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4635) as amended by Law Number 31 of 2014 concerning Amendment to Law Number 13 of 2006 concerning the Protection of Witnesses and Victims (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2014 Number 293, Supplement to the State Gazette Number 5602)
7. Law Number 12 of 2011 concerning the Formation of Laws and Regulations (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2011 Number 82, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5234);
8. Law of the Republic of Indonesia Number 11 of 2012 Concerning the Juvenile Criminal Justice System (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2012 Number 153, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5332);
9. Law Number 23 of 2014 concerning Regional Government (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2014 Number 244, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5587) as amended several times most recently by Law Number 9 of 2015 concerning the Second Amendment to Law Number 23 of 2014 concerning Regional Government (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2015 Number 58, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5679)
10. Government Regulation Number 4 of 2006 concerning the Implementation and Cooperation for the Recovery of Victims of Domestic Violence (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2006 Number 15, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4604);
11. Presidential Regulation Number 8 of 2014 concerning the Protection of Women and Children in Social Conflict (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2014 Number 44);
12. Regional Regulation of Central Java Province Number 3 of 2009 Concerning the Implementation of Protection for Victims of Gender-Based Violence and Children (Regional Gazette Year 2009 Number 3, Supplement to the Regional Gazette number 20);
13. Regional Regulation of Central Java Province Number 7 of 2013 Concerning the Implementation of Child Protection (Regional Gazette Number 2013 Number 7, Supplement to the Regional Gazette Number 53).

With Mutual Approval

Article 8
(1) The obligations and responsibilities of the Regional Government as referred to in Article 7 letter a, include:
a. establishing and implementing policies and programs, and conducting cooperation in activities in the implementation of the protection of women and children from acts of violence;
b. facilitating the establishment of institutions for the protection of women and children from acts of violence and providing support in the form of facilities and infrastructure;
c. allocating a budget for the implementation of the protection of women and children from acts of violence in accordance with the region’s financial capacity;
d. providing guidance and supervision over the implementation of the protection of women and children from acts of violence;
e. providing protection services for women and children who are victims of acts of violence;
f. encouraging and increasing community participation;
g. appointing parents and/or substitute family caregivers as a protection measure for children who become victims of acts of violence.
(2) In order to implement the obligations and responsibilities as referred to in paragraph (1), the Mayor shall stipulate programs and action activities for the protection of women and children in a Regional Action Plan as a basis for Regional Apparatus in implementing the protection of women and children from acts of violence.
(3) The Regional Action Plan as referred to in paragraph (2) constitutes part of the Regional Medium-Term Development Plan.
(4) Further provisions regarding the Regional Action Plan as referred to in paragraph (3) shall be regulated by a Mayor Regulation.

CHAPTER IV
RIGHTS OF WOMEN AND CHILDREN
VICTIMS OF ACTS OF VIOLENCE
Article 5
Women and children victims of acts of violence shall receive the following rights:
a. The right to have their dignity and worth as human beings respected;
b. The right to recovery;
c. The right to determine their own decisions;
d. The right to obtain information;
e. The right to confidentiality;
f. The right to social rehabilitation;
g. The right to complaint handling that is prompt, appropriate, comfortable and in accordance with their needs;
h. The right of victims and their families to obtain facilitation in the judicial process;
i. The right to assistance; and
j. The right to a sense of security.

CHAPTER II
PRINCIPLES AND OBJECTIVES
Article 2
Protection of women and children from acts of violence is implemented based on the principles of:
a. Humanity;
b. Justice and gender equality;
c. Non-discrimination;
d. Order and legal certainty;
e. Transparency;
f. Protection.

CHAPTER IX
GUIDANCE AND SUPERVISION
Article 23
